          "endpoint_field": "toxicity_outcome",
          "endpoint_counts": {
            "Associated with clinical-trial toxicities that led to discontinuation of its development (specific toxicity not described)": 3,
            "Development discontinued because of an unacceptable toxicity profile": 2,
            "Clinical development program was terminated due to toxicity": 2,
            "Development halted due to elevated aminotransferase levels, suggesting liver toxicity": 2,
            "Liver toxicity signal that resulted in discontinuation of development": 2,
            "Development stopped after identification of unacceptable toxicity including phototoxicity, hypoglycaemia, thrombocytopenia and nephritis": 2,
            "ALT elevation >3× ULN (hepatotoxicity); clinical development terminated after hepatotoxicity was observed with repeated use": 2,
            "Development was terminated because of transaminase elevation (hepatotoxicity).": 2,
            "Drug-related grade 3/4 adverse events in 8 of 9 patients; dose-limiting toxicities including dermatitis, hepatitis, QTc prolongation, and arthralgias; dose-limiting toxicity led to early trial closure": 2,
            "Trial terminated early due to toxicities": 2,
            "Clinical development terminated due to severe hepatotoxicity in clinical trials": 2,
            "Phase 1 trial suspended for undisclosed complications related to safety (nature of toxicity not disclosed)": 1,
            "DLTs experienced; in all but one patient the DLT was hepatotoxicity; fatal hepatorenal failure in one patient at 44 mg/m²; poorly tolerated at doses ≥44 mg/m²; most frequent events were gastrointestinal or nervous system events; study terminated due to severe hepatic toxicity": 1,
            "Severe, unexpected dose-related hepatic toxicity; in one patient a liver biopsy showed microvesicular steatosis, periportal inflammation and signs of cholangitis without hepatocyte necrosis; toxicity became unacceptable at 44 mg/m² and led to discontinuation of clinical development": 1,
            "Congestive heart failure / cardiotoxicity: three patients developed significant CHF on the dose-dense regimen; one patient's biopsy showed Grade II anthracycline (doxorubicin) toxicity; high cardiac toxicity rate led to trial closure. Minor hematologic toxicity (one patient required 2 units of packed red blood cells with doxorubicin).": 1,
            "Reversible moderate to severe neurological toxicity manifested by dizziness and unsteady gait; moderate neurotoxicity persisted even after dose reduction; trial closed early due to significant neurotoxicity": 1,
            "Dose-limiting toxicities of nausea/vomiting, hepatic dysfunction, and neuromyotoxicity at higher doses; moderate to severe muscle weakness with elevated creatine phosphokinase and aldolase associated with necrosis and type II muscle fiber atrophy; cardiac toxicity; cumulative fatigue and neuromuscular and cardiac toxicity precluded multiple cycles and led to discontinuation of clinical development": 1,
            "Clinical development was discontinued because of severe adverse effects, including hemorrhagic cystitis": 1,
            "DLTs at level 0 (G4 liver failure, G4 hyponatremia, G3 hyperglycemia, G3 rash and hyponatremia) leading to dose de-escalation; two further DLTs at DL-1 (G3 ALT, G3 neutropenia); grade 3+ hepatotoxicity in four of nine patients; overall grade 3+ toxicity rate of 78%; study prematurely closed due to toxicity": 1,
            "Both phase 1 clinical trials were terminated due to off-target ophthalmological toxicity": 1,
            "thrombocytopenia (abnormally low platelet levels); clinical development halted due to this off-target toxicity": 1,
            "Reversible cardiac events in two patients: one with chest pain and decreased LVEF, one with asymptomatic increase in troponin (cardiotoxicity; further development stopped due to cardiac toxicities)": 1,
            "Grade 3 dose-limiting toxicities of headache, vomiting and back pain in both patients at the 80 mg/week dose level; pain symptoms (headache, back pain, myalgia) were very common and seen in most patients from the first dose level; trial was prematurely terminated due to these severe toxicities and MTD/RP2D could not be identified; sufficient drug exposure to reach active doses could not be achieved.": 1,
            "9/26 (35%) patients experienced severe adverse events leading to treatment discontinuation or death; toxicity-related treatment stops included febrile neutropenia, persistent neutropenia despite two dose reductions, grade 3 thrombocytopenia, asymptomatic LVEF decrease from 52 to 34%, grade 3 diarrhoea, and grade 3 asthenia; grade 3/4 toxicities included neutropenia, septic shock, heart failure, confusion, dyspnoea, nausea/vomiting, and diarrhoea; three toxic deaths (G4 neutropenia, G4 heart failure, septic shock); trial terminated due to unexpectedly high rate of severe adverse events": 1,
            "Hepatotoxicity manifesting as increased alanine transaminase (ALT) elevations greater than 3x ULN, including several Hy's law cases; the phase III trial was terminated because of this toxicity": 1,
            "Severe, reversible hepatotoxicity reported in all five treated patients; no antitumor activity seen; additional clinical studies halted because of the severity of the hepatotoxicity": 1,
            "At 80 mg, DR-6MP was safe and well tolerated; reported AEs included drug-related gastrointestinal disorders, nausea, abdominal pain, decreased appetite, upper abdominal pain, and drug-induced hepatotoxicity (increase in LFTs necessitating dose reduction or study termination); two subjects who had stopped Purinethol due to hepatotoxicity were treated with DR-6MP on compassionate care and stabilized with normal LFTs.": 1,
            "Reported clinical AEs included drug-related gastrointestinal disorders, nausea, abdominal pain, decreased appetite, upper abdominal pain, and drug-induced hepatotoxicity (increase in LFTs necessitating dose reduction or study termination); two subjects had to stop Purinethol treatment due to hepatotoxicity; reduced normal WBC levels relative to DR-6MP.": 1,
            "Severe and prolonged immune-related adverse events when combined with nivolumab; grade ≥3 treatment-emergent AEs including neutropenia, alanine aminotransferase elevation, and liver-related AEs/hepatitis; one treatment-related death from interstitial lung disease occurred in the LET cohort; study terminated early due to safety concerns.": 1,
            "High incidence of immune-related hepatotoxicity; in the abemaciclib plus nivolumab plus ET combination one patient developed fatal ILD; the study was terminated due to toxicity": 1,
            "Higher incidence of severe toxicities versus low-dose arm leading to early trial closure; ≥ grade 2 leukopenia, ≥ grade 2 thrombocytopenia, ≥ grade 2 anemia, grade 2 renal toxicity (5 patients, all on high dose), one transient grade 3 serum creatinine increase, and grade 2 neurotoxicity; the study was closed early because of more episodes of severe toxicity in the high-dose cisplatin arm": 1,
            "Severe CNS neurotoxicity including confusion and disorientation (lasting 4-7 days), stupor, visual/auditory hallucinations, and paranoid psychosis requiring haloperidol; the study was stopped early for accrual because of this toxicity": 1,
            "Of 9 patients, 7 reported at least 1 side effect; 3 patients had akathisia within the first week (2 resolved, 1 led to study termination when it did not resolve with dose reduction); other mild-to-moderate side effects included headache, nausea, dizziness, tiredness and blurred vision, weight gain (10 lb), and increased anxiety; all side effects except weight gain resolved in the 8 patients who finished the study.": 1,
            "Harmful effects (clinical toxicity) that led to the completion/termination of clinical development in Phase III trials for malaria": 1,
            "Severe tenosynovitis in the levofloxacin arm; tenosynovitis persisted for 7 weeks in some patients although the clinical course was favorable in all cases; study was suspended due to this unexpected toxicity, and only 54.5% of randomized patients completed prophylaxis": 1,
            "Trial prematurely closed for toxicity; dose-limiting toxicities (Grade 3 hepatic cytolysis and Grade 3 neutropenia) occurred, including after dose reduction": 1,
            "Phase I/II trials terminated due to severe off-target toxicity (one case of heart failure); most common adverse effect neutropenia; most common grade 3/4 adverse events neutropenia and febrile neutropenia": 1,
            "Severe thrombocytopenia, significant and possibly life-threatening Q-T prolongation, and profound fatigue; clinical development stopped due to severe and unexpected toxicity": 1,
            "Clinical development was halted owing to unpredictable toxicity": 1,
            "Trials terminated early due to overwhelming infectious complications and pulmonary and hepatic toxicity, thought to be immune-mediated": 1,
            "Higher toxicity in the Buparlisib arm; trial stopped prematurely due to higher toxicity and lack of pathologic complete response benefit.": 1,
            "Significant adverse events especially involving ocular function and neurological toxicity; clinical development stopped due to safety concern": 1,
            "3 dose-limiting toxicities in 3 patients (G3 and G4 febrile neutropenia and G3 infection); study terminated early due to toxicities at doses below the anticipated exposure target": 1,
            "Study terminated to new enrollment by the sponsor due to observed (actual) clinical toxicities when MK-5108 was combined with docetaxel in the CT arm at low MK-5108 doses; the toxicities prevented achievement of the targeted therapeutic exposure range.": 1,
            "Dose reductions were required in 4/15 (27%) patients (all in cycle 2, one requiring a second reduction in cycle 3); cycle delays and dose holds were required for 11/15 (73%) patients and were most often related to liver toxicity; the study was terminated due to unacceptable frequency of dosing- schedule adjustments for hepatic toxicity. No DLTs were seen in any cohort.": 1,
            "Dose reductions were required in 6/15 (40%) patients (five in cycle 2 and one in cycle 3); cycle delays and dose holds due to AEs were required for 11/15 (73%) patients, most often related to liver toxicity; the study was terminated due to unacceptable frequency of dosing-schedule adjustments for hepatic toxicity. No DLTs were seen in any cohort.": 1,
            "Glomerulonephritis or nephropathy in 3 patients approximately 8 weeks post-treatment; 2 events judged as possibly related to pafuramidine; development program discontinued due to delayed post-treatment toxicity": 1,
            "Dose-related and dose-limiting local toxicity with pain along the vein of injection and reversible erythema of the surrounding skin; one patient developed asymptomatic bradycardia (60 beats/min) with worsening bradycardia and dizziness requiring infusion interruption; mild leukopenia and reversible grade 1 increases in liver function values (alkaline phosphatase and glutamic pyruvic transaminase); possible neurological disturbances; study closed due to dose-limiting toxicity.": 1,
            "MTD not reached at 80 mg/m² every 3 weeks (accrual continued); a phase I trial was stopped after 4 patients at 75 mg/m² every 3 weeks due to excessive toxicity; MTD was only 30 mg/m²/week in a Japanese phase I trial": 1,
            "Study stopped due to unacceptable toxicity during dose-escalation; MTD not determined; all 19 patients experienced an AE; most considered treatment-related; SAEs, Grade>=3 AEs, DLT AEs, and AEs leading to discontinuation reported": 1
          },
